                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "6f09004d-3bf5-36f6-b613-5539f5dda1d7",
                "word": "chromatic",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "be635564-04fc-3e01-80ba-3c7942695239",
                        "entry_id": "6f09004d-3bf5-36f6-b613-5539f5dda1d7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relating to chromatin (“a complex of DNA, RNA, and proteins within the cell nucleus out of which chromosomes condense during cell division”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3c525c14-a37b-4158-9d88-56f4a9e5b1ad",
                                "sense_id": "be635564-04fc-3e01-80ba-3c7942695239",
                                "text": "Each nucleus has, then, (1) a protoplasmic body, and (2) a portion formed of nuclein, which is the chromatin of [Walther] Flemming. […] The chromatic reticulum of Flemming is only an appearance due to the regular crossing of its convolutions, rarely to their temporary union."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d82e639-71b5-4dd1-8dd4-bb739d05b724",
                                "sense_id": "be635564-04fc-3e01-80ba-3c7942695239",
                                "text": "The chromatic threads next arrange themselves in the equatorial plane of the nucleus, and then divide into two groups of threads, each of which forms a daughter nucleus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bedfbf12-6404-4534-afe9-ab01360b151c",
                                "sense_id": "be635564-04fc-3e01-80ba-3c7942695239",
                                "text": "Prof. [Sydney Howard] Vines also attacks my view of the idioplasmatic nature of the nuclear substance (the chromatic grains); and maintains that it is as easy to speak of the continuity of the cell-body as of that of the nuclear substance, and that the one may transmit heritable qualities to progeny as well as the other."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d1a75d3f-592e-4e5f-85b6-07c3483950c2",
                                "sense_id": "be635564-04fc-3e01-80ba-3c7942695239",
                                "text": "If we are amazed at the precision with which the chromatic elements of the nucleus are divided and distributed, we can be no less astonished at the wonderful directive influence exercised by the asters upon the nuclei; […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
